<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/7th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Marj Rahit (684)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">684</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-fitna">Second Fitna</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Marj Rahit was fought in August 684 north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> between the Umayyad Marwan I, backed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-kalb">Kalb</a> tribes, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qays">Qays</a> tribes under al-Dahhak ibn Qays, who supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-allah-ibn-al-zubayr">Ibn al-Zubayr</a>. Marwan's victory secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-fitna">Second Fitna</a> and fuelled a long feud between the Qays and Kalb tribal factions.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the death of Mu'awiya I in 680, the succession of his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazid-i">Yazid I</a> was contested, chiefly by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husayn-ibn-ali">Husayn ibn Ali</a>, killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-karbala">Battle of Karbala</a> in October 680, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-allah-ibn-al-zubayr">Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr</a>, who denounced Umayyad rule from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>. Yazid's death in November 683 ended a siege of Mecca, and his son Mu'awiya II died a few weeks into his reign. Umayyad authority then collapsed. Most of the Muslim world recognised Ibn al-Zubayr as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, including the Qaysi tribes of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and the Jazira, the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a>, Nu'man ibn Bashir, and Natil ibn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qays">Qays</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>.</p><p>The pro-Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-kalb">Banu Kalb</a> under Ibn Bahdal, dominant in the district of Jordan, held an assembly of the loyal tribes at Jabiya, which recognised Marwan ibn al-Hakam, a distant cousin of Mu'awiya I and once an aide of Caliph Uthman, as caliph. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qays">Qays</a> rallied to the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, al-Dahhak ibn Qays al-Fihri, who declared for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-allah-ibn-al-zubayr">Ibn al-Zubayr</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>A revolt led by the Ghassanid noble Yazid ibn Abi al-Nims secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> for Marwan. The armies first met in mid-July 684 at Marj al-Suffar, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qays">Qays</a> were pushed back to Marj Rahit, about 17 kilometres north-east of Damascus. Twenty days of skirmishing ended in the decisive battle on 18 August. The traditions give different numbers but agree that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> were considerably outnumbered. The course of the fighting is unclear, but al-Dahhak and the main Qaysi leaders fell. Nikita Elisséeff explains the victory by defections from the Qays and by Umayyad control of the treasury in Damascus. The survivors fled to Qarqisiya under Zufar ibn al-Harith al-Kilabi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Marwan was proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and recovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> later that year. His son Abd al-Malik, caliph from April 685, brought Zufar's tribes back in 691, defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musab-ibn-al-zubayr">Mus'ab ibn al-Zubayr</a>, and in October 692 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-allah-ibn-al-zubayr">Ibn al-Zubayr</a> was killed after another siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>. The battle hardened the rivalry between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qays">Qays</a> and Yaman, which divided the caliphate's tribes into two blocs and helped bring on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-fitna">Third Fitna</a>. Julius Wellhausen judged that Marj Rahit brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> victory and at the same time shattered the foundations of their power.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Marj Rahit (684)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Umayyad victory at <b>Marj Rahit</b> in 684 kept Syria, Marwan I retook Egypt in 685, and Abd al-Malik reconquered Iraq, where Mus'ab was killed in 691.</blockquote><a href="/state/caliphate-of-ibn-al-zubayr">Caliphate of Ibn al-Zubayr</a></li></ul></section>
